            <ArticleTitle>A case of painless arms/moving fingers responsive to botulinum toxin a injections.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>55-6</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Painless arms/moving fingers is a rare variant of the painful limbs/moving extremities syndrome (PLME) characterized by slow involuntary movements of the toes or fingers. Its typical form is associated with limb pain. We report the first case of painless arms/moving fingers and describe its favorable response to botulinum toxin A injections. The presence of dystonic symptoms in our case argues in favor of a possible implication of a dystonic mechanism in at least some cases of PLME.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
